{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-154041",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The variable $z$ is being *divided* by $6.$ To isolate $z$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-6$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-\\frac{z}{6} \\\\ (-6)\\cdot 4\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\,\\, & >(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >(\\cancel{-}6)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{6z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{\\cancel{6}z}{\\cancel{6}} \\\\ -24 & >z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nz<-24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z<-24.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
